course: biology
lesson: 67
title: Digestion: the journey of a sandwich
level: primary-4
stars: 2
explain: A cheese sandwich at lunch goes on a long journey, and by the end your body has broken it into pieces so tiny that they pass into your blood.
explain: It starts in the mouth: teeth cut and grind, and saliva wets the food and already begins turning the starch of the bread into sugar, which is why bread chewed for a long time tastes sweeter.
explain: When you swallow, muscles in the food pipe squeeze it down in waves, like toothpaste pushed along a tube, so it would reach your stomach even if you stood on your head.
explain: The stomach churns it in strong acid, the long folded small intestine soaks up the goodness, and the large intestine takes back the water before what is left leaves the body.
explain: Along the way the liver works as the body's chemical factory, fibre from apple peel keeps things moving, and fizzy drinks come back up as burps.
fact: Teeth cut and grind food into small pieces, which are easier to swallow and quicker to digest, and saliva wets it and starts breaking the starch in bread into sugar.
fact: Smelling or even thinking about tasty food makes glands in the mouth release saliva, and we make roughly 1 litre of saliva a day.
fact: Food is pushed down the food pipe, the oesophagus, by waves of muscle squeezing, so swallowing works even upside down, and astronauts can swallow in space.
fact: When you swallow, a flap called the epiglottis covers the windpipe; if you laugh or talk while swallowing, some drink can slip into the windpipe and you cough it out.
fact: The stomach churns food with strong hydrochloric acid and enzymes that break down proteins, and the acid kills many germs; a layer of mucus stops it digesting itself.
fact: A rumbling tummy is the stomach and gut squeezing even when nearly empty, pushing air and juices around.
fact: The small intestine is about 6 to 7 metres long in an adult, folded up in the tummy, and most digestion and absorption of food into the blood happens there.
fact: The inside of the small intestine is covered in millions of tiny finger-like folds called villi, which give it a huge surface for soaking up food.
fact: The large intestine soaks up water and salts from what is left, and its bacteria break down some of the fibre.
fact: Fibre from fruit, vegetables and whole grains is not digested by us but helps food move along the gut and feeds the good gut bacteria.
fact: Fizzy drinks are full of carbon dioxide gas, which collects in the stomach and comes back up as a burp.
fact: The liver is the largest organ inside the body; it processes food from the gut, stores energy and vitamins, makes bile to help digest fat and cleans harmful substances from the blood.
fact: The whole journey of a meal through the gut usually takes about one to three days.
fact: Swallowed chewing gum is not digested, but it does not stay inside for 7 years: it passes through and leaves the body in a few days, like other things we cannot digest.
exercise: What do your teeth do to a sandwich? | Cut and grind it into small pieces | Suck all the goodness out of it | Turn it straight into blood at once | Melt it with the heat of the mouth
exercise: What does saliva start to do to the bread? | Break its starch down into sugar | Turn the bread into fat for later | Turn it into a strong stomach acid | Cool it down ready for the stomach
exercise: Why does your mouth water when you smell pizza? | Smelling tasty food makes glands release saliva | The smell of pizza is mostly made of water drops | Your teeth are melting a little | Your nose is dripping down into your mouth
exercise: How does food get from your mouth to your stomach? | Muscles in the food pipe squeeze it down in waves | It simply falls down because of gravity | The stomach sucks it down like a straw | Tiny hairs carry it down one crumb at a time
exercise: Could you swallow food standing on your head? | Yes, because the food pipe muscles push it along | No, the food would fall straight back out of your mouth | Only if you drink a lot of water with it | Only if you are a trained astronaut
exercise: What does the epiglottis do? | Covers the windpipe when you swallow | Grinds food into paste in the stomach | Makes saliva in the mouth | Stores spare food inside the liver
exercise: Why do you cough when a drink goes down the wrong way? | Some drink slipped into the windpipe and you are clearing it | Your stomach is too full and is pushing the drink back up | The drink was too cold for your teeth | Your lungs want more drink and are asking for it
exercise: Why does your tummy rumble before lunch? | The empty gut squeezes and pushes air and juices around | Your stomach is talking to your brain in real words | Your heart is beating down inside your tummy | Your hip bones are rubbing together when you are hungry
exercise: What is inside the stomach that helps digest food? | Strong acid and enzymes | Rows of tiny sharp teeth | Cold water and nothing else | Only air and nothing else
exercise: Why doesn't the stomach digest itself? | A layer of mucus protects its lining | The acid is far too weak to hurt anything at all | The stomach wall is made of hard bone | The stomach stays empty most of the time
exercise: Where does most digestion and soaking up of food happen? | In the small intestine | In the mouth | In the large intestine | In the liver
exercise: How long is an adult's small intestine? | About 6 to 7 metres | About 6 to 7 centimetres | About 60 to 70 metres | About as long as your arm
exercise: Where does most of the water left in food get soaked up? | In the large intestine | In the mouth, under the tongue | In the lungs, as we breathe | In the heart and blood vessels
exercise: Why is it good to eat an apple with its peel on? | The peel has fibre that keeps food moving along the gut | The peel holds nearly all of the apple's sugar and sweetness | The peel makes the apple heavier and more filling | The peel stops you burping after you eat
exercise: Why do you burp after a fizzy drink? | Carbon dioxide gas from the drink comes back up | The drink turns into air once it is in the stomach | Your stomach is angry with all the sugar | The bubbles go down into your lungs and back out
exercise: Which is the largest organ inside the body? | The liver | The beating heart | The stomach | The big brain
exercise: What happens to chewing gum if you swallow it? | It passes through and leaves the body in a few days | It stays stuck in your tummy for exactly seven years | It wraps itself around your heart | It turns into a little gum tree inside you
test: mum says if i swallow gum it stays in me for 7 years. true or nah? | Nah, it passes through and leaves in a few days | True, it sticks to the stomach wall for 7 whole years | True, but only if it is bubblegum | Nah, it melts in your mouth before you swallow
test: In a cartoon a sandwich slides down a slide straight into an empty stomach and stays whole. Put it right. | It is chewed, mixed with saliva, pushed down by muscles and broken down by acid | Sandwiches really do reach the stomach whole and stay whole there until bedtime | Food goes to the lungs first and only then on to the stomach | The stomach has teeth of its own that chew the sandwich up
test: What would happen if you did not chew a sandwich at all? | It would be harder to swallow and slower to digest | It would digest faster because it is still in one piece | It would go straight into the blood | It would never reach the stomach
test: Raj chews a piece of bread for a whole minute and it starts to taste sweet. Why? | Saliva is turning the starch in the bread into sugar | His teeth are slowly adding sugar to the bread as he chews | The bread was sweet all along but hidden | His tongue is getting tired of the taste
test: Ella laughs while drinking juice and coughs and splutters. What happened? | The epiglottis did not cover her windpipe in time and juice slipped in | The juice went down into her stomach far too fast and bounced back up | Her teeth bit the juice by mistake | The juice was far too sweet for her throat
test: ugh, imagine the stomach acid melting your whole tummy. why doesn't it? | A layer of mucus protects the stomach lining | The acid is just water so it cannot melt anything | The stomach is made of steel lol | The acid only switches on at night
test: Why is the small intestine so long and folded? | A long, folded tube with villi gives a huge surface to soak up food | It needs to be long enough to reach right down to the feet and back | Long tubes make the food taste better | It stores spare food in its folds for the winter
test: Sam eats only white bread and sweets for a week and his tummy feels stuck. What is missing? | Fibre from fruit, vegetables and whole grains | More fizzy drinks to wash things through | More salt on his chips | Nothing, sweets already have plenty of fibre in them
test: At lunch a friend says what you eat today is all gone by tea time. Right? | No, a meal usually takes about one to three days to go through | Yes, food is used up completely in about two hours | Yes, by tea time it has all turned into breath and gone | No, food stays inside you for a whole month or more
test: Why do astronauts not need gravity to swallow their food? | Muscles in the food pipe push the food along by squeezing | They always eat upside down so it all evens out | Space food floats into the stomach by itself | They drink all their food through tubes into the stomach
test: In a game, eating a mushroom gives instant energy the second it touches your lips. How long does real food take? | Some time, because it must be digested and soaked into the blood first | It really does work the instant it touches the mouth, just like in the game | Energy goes in through the teeth | Energy only arrives after a whole year
exam: Identify the principal site of the absorption of digested food into the blood. | The small intestine | The oesophagus | The large intestine | The mouth
exam: Which of the following statements most accurately characterises the passage of food along the oesophagus? | It is propelled by waves of muscular contraction | It descends solely under the influence of gravity | It is drawn downward by suction from the stomach | It is conveyed downward by repeated movements of the epiglottis
exam: It is asserted that chewing gum, once swallowed, persists within the stomach for seven years. Select the response consistent with the established facts. | The assertion is false; gum is excreted within a few days | The assertion is true of all varieties of gum | The assertion is true of gum swallowed in childhood | The assertion is true where the gum has not been chewed thoroughly
exam: Ascertain the function of the epiglottis during the act of swallowing. | It covers the windpipe, preventing the entry of food | It secretes acid into the stomach during swallowing | It grinds food prior to swallowing | It absorbs water from the food as it passes downward
